# The Okular Project License

**Version 1.0 — the license of the Okular compiler, its source code, and
the Okular project materials.**

This is a source-available license. You may study, use, and build with the
Okular project. You may not redistribute it or publish forks of it.
Software you create **with** Okular is yours — completely unrestricted.

This file is written to be read by humans. It is the license of the
project itself; section 6 explains exactly how it applies (and does not
apply) to the software you build with the compiler.

---

## 1. Definitions

In this license:

* **The Project** means the Okular programming language project: the
  compiler, its source code, the runtime library source, the
  specification, the documentation, the examples, the test suite, the
  build tooling, and the distribution packages published by the Project,
  as a whole and in any version.
* **Project Source** means the source code and other files of the
  Project, in original or modified form.
* **The Compiler** means any native executable built from the Project
  Source that translates Okular programs into native code.
* **The Runtime Library** means the parts of the Project that the
  Compiler compiles *into* programs as part of normal compilation — at
  the time of writing, the runtime module (`rt.ok` and anything the
  Project designates as runtime library or standard-library code that is
  linked into compiled programs by default).
* **Your Software** means a program or library that you (or anyone)
  write in the Okular language — the source code *you* author — together
  with the native binaries the Compiler produces from that source.
* **Redistribution** means making the Project, or a copy or modified
  version of the Project, available to any third party: publishing it,
  hosting it for download, shipping it inside another product as the
  Okular compiler/toolchain, mirroring it as an distribution of its own,
  or selling it.
* **A Fork** means a derivative of the Project presented as an
  independent or alternative project — the same or similar compiler,
  language, or toolchain under another name or another home, whether
  derived by copying the Project Source, by reusing its design and
  materials wholesale, or by continued development of a modified copy.

## 2. What you MAY do with the Project

* **View and study** the Project Source, the specification, and every
  other file, for any purpose, including education and research.
* **Use the Compiler** — including the released native binaries — to
  compile, build, test, and run software, commercially or not.
* **Build Your Software** and do anything whatsoever with it. See
  section 5: Your Software is not restricted by this license.
* **Modify the Project for your own internal use** — to experiment, to
  port internally, to debug, to measure. Modifications you keep to
  yourself (and to your organization's internal machines) are permitted.
* **Quote and discuss** the Project: small excerpts for review,
  teaching, bug reports, and discussion are fine — that is not
  Redistribution of the Project.
* **Contribute** to the Project through its accepted contribution
  channels (see section 7).

## 3. What you may NOT do with the Project

* **No Redistribution.** You may not redistribute the Project or Project
  Source — original or modified — as a distribution of your own: no
  publishing copies, no hosting mirrors, no bundling the compiler into
  another product as its Okular toolchain, no selling it.
* **No Forks.** You may not create or distribute a Fork. Publishing an
  alternative or renamed compiler, language, or toolchain derived from
  this Project — in whole or in substantial part — is not permitted.
* **No misleading origin.** You may not represent work derived from this
  Project as your own independent creation.
* **No removal of this license or of attribution** notices the Project
  carries.

If you want to redistribute or build on the Project beyond these terms,
contact the Project's maintainer for a separate written agreement.

## 4. Clarity about what counts (and does not count)

* Installing the Compiler on machines you administer — including build
  machines and CI runners — is **use**, not Redistribution.
* Compiling Your Software with the Compiler, and distributing the
  resulting binaries, is **not** Redistribution of the Project. It does
  not make Your Software part of the Project or subject to this license.
* Sending Project Source excerpts in a bug report, mailing list, or
  discussion is **not** Redistribution.
* A backup copy kept for your own recovery purposes is **not**
  Redistribution.
* Publishing a *re-packaged Okular distribution* — even with additions —
  **is** Redistribution. Publishing a compiler derived from this one —
  same language, same design, different name — **is** a Fork.

## 5. Your Software is yours (the compiled-output rule)

**This license does not restrict software built with the Compiler.**

The Okular Project claims no ownership, control, or revenue interest in
Your Software. You may distribute, sell, publish, open-source, or keep
proprietary the programs and libraries you write in Okular and the
binaries produced from them — commercially or non-commercially, with any
license you choose, or with none.

Compiling with Okular does not make Your Software a derivative
distribution of the Project, and does not subject Your Software to this
license.

## 6. The Runtime Library exception

Normal compilation with the Compiler links the Runtime Library into Your
Software (today: the runtime module `rt.ok`).

**The Runtime Library, as compiled into Your Software, is subject to no
restriction from this license.** For the purpose of Your Software, you
may treat the compiled Runtime Library as if it carried a permissive,
attribution-free license: use, modify, and distribute Your Software
containing it without restriction, including commercially.

This exception exists precisely so that section 5 is true in practice,
not only on paper: the presence of the compiled Runtime Library inside
Your Software's binaries never makes Your Software a derivative
distribution of the Project.

The exception covers only the Runtime Library as embedded in Your
Software. Taking the Runtime Library source itself, or any other Project
Source, and distributing it as standalone source or as part of another
compiler/toolchain project remains subject to sections 2 and 3.

## 7. Contributions

Contributions you submit for inclusion in the Project are accepted under
this license, and under the terms of this license only. You retain the
copyright in your contribution; you grant the Project the perpetual,
worldwide right to use, modify, and distribute it as part of the Project
under this license. Do not submit work you are not entitled to license
this way.

A contribution accepted into the Project becomes part of the Project
Source and is covered by sections 2 and 3 like the rest of the Project —
including the Runtime Library exception when the contribution lands in
the Runtime Library.

## 8. Third-party components

The Project may contain or make use of third-party components. Those
components keep their own licenses, which remain in force for those
components. This license applies to the Project's own work and does not
override, replace, or relicense third-party work.

